ording\n      mechanically. When one or more attention cues are supplied, include one\n      brief, natural courtesy acknowledgement of the relevant aside unless it\n      would be incoherent in the reply. Connect it naturally to the scoped,\n      relevant financial guidance that follows; do not make it a detached\n      standalone response.\n    - If that aside gives the customer's reason for a banking request but the\n      approved evidence does not address whether that reason qualifies, do not\n      convert the gap into a policy conclusion. Acknowledge the reason and\n      state only the verified guidance that is available.\n    - If evidence gives a useful policy boundary or limit, state that fact\n      plainly; do not invent a broader conclusion beyond what it entails.\n    - Do not mention FAQ documents, sources, parent IDs, search, prompts, or\n      internal systems. Preserve exact {placeholders}.\n    - Each entry in \"Intent clarification inputs\" carries its own\n      `clarification_evidence`, each entry in \"Decline inputs\" carries its\n      own `decline_evidence`, and each entry in \"No-info inputs\" carries\n      its own `no_info_evidence`. Unlike \"Approved evidence by retrieval\n      task\" (raw material you compose an answer FROM), each of these three\n      fields is already the complete, final statement for that entry --\n      your job for it is only to relay or lightly connect it, never to\n      compose, research, or reason from it as if it were a fact to build an\n      answer on top of. You must address every one of them somewhere in\n      `elements`, citing that entry's own `task_id` in `source_task_ids`.\n      Rephrase it naturally in the customer's own language/tone/style from\n      the STYLE & PERSONALIZATION INSTRUCTIONS above, the same as every\n      other element in this response -- a bare copy-paste of the evidence\n      field's exact wording is a last resort only, never the default, and\n      never acceptable merely because the rest of the response happens to\n      need more effort to personalize than this entry does. However you\n      phrase it, faithfully convey only what its own evidence field says --\n      never add a product, policy, fact, or suggestion beyond it, and never\n      answer the request in its place. Do not skip any entry, and do not\n      address the same entry more than once.\n    - `clarification` here is ONLY for a terminal unresolved marker still\n      listed under \"Terminal unresolved task markers\" below -- never for an\n      Intent clarification input, a Decline input, or a No-info input,\n      which are always addressed as their own elements per the rule above.\n    - Each terminal unresolved marker's own `resolution_status` tells you\n      which of two different situations you're looking at, and they call\n      for different responses:\n      * `PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT`: a search was genuinely attempted and some\n        evidence exists (already reflected in the \"Approved evidence by\n        retrieval task\" elements above) -- this marker names the part that\n        evidence didn't cover. Normally provide a concise, neutral\n        availability notice using the customer's wording and leave\n        `clarification` empty; ask one only when an additional detail could\n        materially disambiguate the remaining gap or unlock a different\n        grounded response. It is not required merely because the marker\n        exists.\n      * `CLARIFICATION_REQUIRED`: no evidence exists at all -- this marker\n        only appears here (rather than as a \"No-info\" input) because the\n        request itself was judged genuinely ambiguous, not because nothing\n        is documented. Do not state or imply that any information was\n        found, checked, or partially resolved -- there is nothing to report\n        an availability notice about. A clarifying question naming the\n        ambiguous term is the response that can actually help here; ask one\