urpose code SALA) during the month.\\nAny amount exceeding {{faq_bf86ef1f_010}} earns only the base rate of {{faq_bf86ef1f_011}} p.a. Interest is posted on the final day of each month.\\n\\n**Illustrative calculation (December, {{faq_bf86ef1f_012}}‑day month):**\\nAssume you maintain a daily balance of {{faq_bf86ef1f_013}}, complete five qualifying card purchases of at least {{faq_bf86ef1f_014}} each on {{faq_bf86ef1f_015}} Dec, and have a {{faq_bf86ef1f_016}} salary credit on {{faq_bf86ef1f_017}} Dec.\\n- **Union member scenario**\\n  - Base on first {{faq_bf86ef1f_018}} M: {{faq_bf86ef1f_019}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_020}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_021}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_022}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_023}}\\n  - Spend‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_024}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_025}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_026}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_027}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_028}}\\n  - Balance‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_029}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_030}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_031}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_032}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_033}}\\n  - Salary‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_034}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_035}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_036}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_037}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_038}}\\n  - Base on excess {{faq_bf86ef1f_039}}: {{faq_bf86ef1f_040}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_041}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_042}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_043}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_044}}\\n  - **Total interest:** {{faq_bf86ef1f_045}}\\n- **Non‑Union member scenario**\\n  - Base on first {{faq_bf86ef1f_046}} M: {{faq_bf86ef1f_047}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_048}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_049}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_050}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_051}}\\n  - Spend‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_052}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_053}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_054}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_055}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_056}}\\n  - Balance‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_057}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_058}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_059}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_060}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_061}}\\n  - Salary‑bonus: {{faq_bf86ef1f_062}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_063}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_064}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_065}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_066}}\\n  - Base on excess {{faq_bf86ef1f_067}}: {{faq_bf86ef1f_068}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_069}} × {{faq_bf86ef1f_070}}/{{faq_bf86ef1f_071}} = {{faq_bf86ef1f_072}}\\n  - **Total interest:** {{faq_bf86ef1f_073}}\\n\\nThese figures are simplified for demonstration purposes.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_353df275\", \"content\": \"Question: What interest can I expect to earn on a Horizon Bank Savings Account?\\n\\nAnswer: - The rate you receive is tied to the savings plan you are enrolled in. If you have not selected a plan, Horizon Bank automatically places you in the Signature plan.\\n- Under the Signature plan you may earn a combined interest of up to {{faq_353df275_001}} per annum on the first {{faq_353df275_002}} of your savings balance. The rate is broken down as follows:\\n  * Base interest: {{faq_353df275_003}} p.a. (no conditions).\\n  * Spend bonus: {{faq_353df275_004}} p.a. for Horizon Union members or {{faq_353df275_005}} p.a. for non‑members, provided you complete at least five qualifying card transactions each month.\\n  * Balance bonus: {{faq_353df275_006}} p.a. when your average daily balance for the month reaches {{faq_353df275_007}}.\\n  * Salary bonus: {{faq_353df275_008}} p.a. if a minimum salary credit of {{faq_353df275_009}} is posted in a single GIRO transaction with purpose code SALA during the month.\\n- Any amount above {{faq_353df275_010}} accrues only the base rate of {{faq_353df275_011}} p.a.\\n- Interest is credited on the last day of each month.\\n\\n**Illustrative example (December, {{faq_353df275_012}}‑day month)**\\nAssume you maintain a daily balance of {{faq_353df275_013}}, execute five qualifying card purchases of at least {{faq_353df275_014}} each on {{faq_353df275_015}} Dec, and receive a {{faq_353df275_016}} salary credit on {{faq_353df275_017}} Dec.\\n\\n*For Horizon Union members:*\\n  - Base on first {{faq_353df275_018}} M: {{faq_353df275_019}} × {{faq_353df275_020}} × {{faq_353df275_021}}/{{faq_353df275_022}} = {{faq_353df275_023}}\\n  - Spend bonus: {{faq_353df275_024}} × {{faq_353df275_025}} × {{faq_353df275_026}}/{{faq_353df275_027}} = {{faq_353df275_028}}\\n  - Balance bonus: {{faq_353df275_029}} × {{faq_353df275_030}} × {{faq_353df275_031}}/{{faq_353df275_032}} = {{faq_353df275_033}}\\n  - Salary bonus: {{faq_353df275_034}} × {{faq_353df275_035}} × {{faq_353df275_036}}/{{faq_353df275_037}} = {{faq_353df275_038}}\\n  - Base on excess {{faq_353df275_039}}: {{faq_353df275_040}} × {{faq_353df275_041}} × {{faq_353df275_042}}/{{faq_353df275_043}} = {{faq_353df275_044}}\\n  - **Total interest = {{faq_353df275_045}}**\\n\\n*For non‑Union members:*\\n  - Base on first {{faq_353df275_046}} M: {{faq_353df275_047}} × {{faq_353df275_048}} × {{faq_353df275_049}}/{{faq_353df275_050}} = {{faq_353df275_051}}\\n  - Spend bonus: {{faq_353df275_052}} × {{faq_353df275_053}} × {{faq_353df275_054}}/{{faq_353df275_055}} = {{faq_353df275_056}}\\n  - Balance bonus: {{faq_353df275_057}} × {{faq_353df275_058}} × {{faq_353df275_059}}/{{faq_353df275_060}} = {{faq_353df275_061}}\\n  - Salary bonus: {{faq_353df275_062}} × {{faq_353df275_063}} × {{faq_353df275_064}}/{{faq_353df275_065}} = {{faq_353df275_066}}\\n  - Base on excess {{faq_353df275_067}}: {{faq_353df275_068}} × {{faq_353df275_069}} × {{faq_353df275_070}}/{{faq_353df275_071}} = {{faq_353df275_072}}\\n  - **Total interest = {{faq_353df275_073}}**\\n\\n*Note: The figures above are simplified for illustration purposes.*\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_07c3da01\", \"content\": \"Question: When does Horizon Bank credit interest to my account?\\n\\nAnswer: - On the final day of each month.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_7b5e0f27\", \"content\": \"Question: What three savings‑plan choices does Horizon Bank provide for its deposit accounts?\\n\\nAnswer: - Horizon Bank now lets you select a savings plan that determines the interest applied to the balance in your savings account. The three available options are **Signature**, **Zen**, and **Flex**.\\n- You may switch your plan at any time before the month ends; the new selection takes effect on the {{faq_7b5e0f27_001}}st day of the following month.\\n- Each successful change triggers an in‑app push notification and an email alert.\\n- To see which plan is currently active, open the **Horizon App**, navigate to **Savings > Interest This Month**, where you can also monitor your progress toward any bonus interest tied to the chosen plan.\\n- If you do not make a change during a given month, the existing plan automatically rolls over into the next month.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_fc5a7921\", \"content\": \"Question: What is the schedule for interest credits on my Horizon Bank deposit account?\\n\\nAnswer: - Interest is posted to the account on a monthly basis.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_01a85fa7\", \"content\": \"Question: What is the schedule for interest credits on my Horizon Bank deposit account?\\n\\nAnswer: - Interest is posted to the account on a monthly basis.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_ad98a5ba\", \"content\": \"Question: What are the details of Horizon Bank's Signature deposit plan?\\n\\nAnswer: - The Horizon Signature account provides a maximum annual interest of {{faq_ad98a5ba_001}} on the first {{faq_ad98a5ba_002}} million of your balance, composed of:\\n  • A base rate of {{faq_ad98a5ba_003}} per year, applied without any conditions;\\n  • A spend‑bonus of {{faq_ad98a5ba_004}} per year for Horizon Union members (or {{faq_ad98a5ba_005}} for non‑members) when you complete five qualifying {{faq_ad98a5ba_006}} card transactions in a month;\\n  • A balance‑bonus of {{faq_ad98a5ba_007}} per year once the account maintains an average daily balance of at least {{faq_ad98a5ba_008}} during the month;\\n  • A salary‑bonus of {{faq_ad98a5ba_009}} per year if a single GIRO credit of a minimum of {{faq_ad98a5ba_010}} is posted in the month.\\n- Any portion of the balance that exceeds {{faq_ad98a5ba_011}} million earns only the base rate of {{faq_ad98a5ba_012}} per year.\\n- Interest is credited to the account on the last day of each month.\"}]}]\n\n        Rules:\n        - Each Task Input contains `task.customer_component`: the intent\n          formulator's resolved sub-query for that task. Evaluate a task ONLY\n          against its own `customer_component` and that task's candidate\n          parents. It also contains `task.customer_original_wording`: the\n          customer's own faithful wording for this task, which may be\n          plainer or vaguer than `customer_component` (which can be\n          elaborated with retrieval-oriented wording the customer never\n          actually said). Use `customer_original_wording`, never\n          `customer_component`, as the source when you set `unresolved_term`\n          below -- a clarifying question must quote something the customer\n          will recognize as their own.\n        - Each Task Input may include `prior_sufficiency_audit_history` from\n          earlier attempts for the same task in this turn. Read it before\n          judging the current candidates and remain consistent with its prior\n          sufficiency judgments. This history is internal audit context only;\n          it is not customer-facing evidence and does not authorize any new\n          factual claim.\n        - Classify each task as SUFFICIENT, PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT, or\n          INSUFFICIENT. Judge whether the supplied parents provide useful,\n          materially relevant information for the task—not whether they repeat\n          the customer's wording or explicitly resolve every possible\n          interpretation of the customer's question.\n        - PARTIAL-EVIDENCE DECISION ORDER: First identify whether any supplied\n          parent contains a factual proposition directly relevant to a\n          meaningful aspect of the customer's resolved request. If one does,\n          do not return INSUFFICIENT merely because the parent cannot determine\n          the customer's requested outcome, permission, eligibility,\n          exception, legality, preferred action, or other broader conclusion.\n          Retain that parent as PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT when its supported fact\n          can be truthfully presented as useful, bounded Horizon guidance\n          while another material aspect remains unresolved. Continue retrieval\n          for the unresolved aspect.\n        - Return INSUFFICIENT only when no supplied parent contains any\n          factual proposition that can be truthfully and materially presented\n          in relation to the resolved request. A parent is not material merely\n          because it concerns the same product: its supported fact must\n          directly concern the same topic, condition, process, boundary, or\n          factual premise in the customer's request.\n        - The reverse matters just as much: a parent whose own subject is a\n          distinctly-named, different product or service is not material\n          merely because it is credit/loan-adjacent, belongs to the same\n          broader family, or is reached through the same in-app menu path as\n          the customer's named product. For example, a balance transfer\n          draws on loan credit and is filed under the app's \"Loan\" section\n          alongside a personal loan application -- but a parent describing\n          how to request a balance transfer does not materially answer a\n          request about applying for a (personal/general) loan. Do not let\n          shared category vocabulary or menu wording (\"Loan section\",\n          \"commence your application\") substitute for the parent actually\n          being about the specific product or process the customer named.\n          Only treat a differently-named product's parent as material when\n          it explicitly states it also applies to, or is the general form\n          of, the customer's named product.\n        - SUFFICIENT: the selected parents contain enough grounded information\n          for a useful response to the `customer_component`, even when that\n          response is an explicit policy boundary, limit, condition,\n          restriction, or other related factual information rather than a\n          literal yes/no answer. The response generator will state only what\n          the evidence supports and must not invent a broader conclusion.\n        \nPRECISION REFERENCE CONTRACT:\n- A token matching `{{faq_*}}` is a database-backed exact-value reference,\n  never missing, unknown, incomplete, or a customer-visible template.\n- It may represent an exact amount, rate, limit, date, threshold, or other\n  precise value. Treat it as the underlying approved fact for relevance,\n  sufficiency, generation, compliance, and groundedness.\n- Preserve the token exactly in any draft. The backend deterministically\n  substitutes its authoritative value only after outbound validation.\n- Never reject, weaken, or describe evidence as unavailable merely because the\n  exact value is represented by a `{{faq_*}}` token.\n- Within the stated scope, \"up to {{faq_*}}\" expresses an exact maximum. It\n  supports the bounded conclusion that the stated coverage or benefit does not\n  exceed that value. It does not, by itself, support claims about applications,\n  approvals, denials, exceptions, or who controls the limit.\n\n        - PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT: selected parents provide grounded, material\n          information for one meaningful part of the `customer_component`,\n          while another material part has no useful related evidence. Select\n          only the supporting parent IDs; the backend will retain them while\n          retrieval continues.\n        - INSUFFICIENT: the supplied parents provide no grounded, materially\n          relevant information that could be usefully presented in response to\n          the `customer_component`.\n        - For INSUFFICIENT, also set `likely_ambiguous_request`: true only\n          when the retrieval genuinely failed because `customer_component`\n          itself is under-specified or could reasonably mean one of several\n          different things (e.g. \"the fee\" without naming which fee, \"the\n          loan\" without naming which loan type) -- the request needs more\n          detail before any evidence search could succeed. False when the\n          request was already specific and clear but the documentation\n          simply does not cover it (a real coverage gap, not an ambiguity).\n          Always false for SUFFICIENT or PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT. This decides\n          whether the backend later offers the customer a clarifying\n          question instead of a flat \"not available\" statement -- reserve\n          true for genuine ambiguity, not merely for \"no matching content.\"\n        - CLARIFICATION AUTHORING: you are the node closest to the actual\n          evidence gap, so YOU decide whether a clarifying question is\n          warranted and what to ask -- the backend only relays what you set\n          here verbatim, it makes no separate judgment call of its own.\n          Set `unresolved_term` (else leave it as an empty string) in\n          exactly two cases:\n          (a) status is PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT and the unresolved part is\n              something an additional customer detail could plausibly\n              disambiguate or unlock a materially different answer for --\n              not merely \"more evidence would be nice.\" A gap that's simply\n              undocumented, where no customer detail would change the\n              outcome, does not warrant this; leave it empty and let the\n              backend's own neutral availability notice handle it instead.\n          (b) status is INSUFFICIENT and you just set likely_ambiguous_request\n              to true. In this case always set unresolved_term too --\n              naming the specific ambiguous part is exactly what\n              likely_ambiguous_request=true already asserts.\n          `unresolved_term` must be a concise phrase naming exactly the\n          ambiguous or unresolved part, drawn from `customer_original_wording`\n          -- never from `customer_component` (see above), and never the\n          whole thing restated unless the whole thing is genuinely what's\n          unresolved.\n        - When `unresolved_term` is non-empty, also set\n          `clarification_candidate_replies`: 0-3 plausible guesses at what\n          the customer likely means, most likely first, using Trusted\n          Conversation Context and the specific candidate parents you just\n          reviewed as your grounding (e.g. two retrieved parents cover\n          different sub-topics the customer might have meant; the customer\n          named a related product two turns ago) -- never the Product/Intent\n          Matrix directly, that is not this call's job. Leave empty when you\n          have no real, statable basis to guess, even though the question\n          itself is still worth asking. Each entry needs a short \"label\"\n          (chip text), a \"message\" that is a complete, natural sentence a\n          customer would actually send, and a \"reason\": a concise,\n          internal-only justification naming the specific evidence or\n          conversation detail behind that guess. A guess you cannot state a\n          concrete reason for is not genuinely inferable -- leave it out.\n        - Use the ordinary meaning of explicit policy boundaries, quantities,\n          conditions, and restrictions when deciding whether a task is\n          response-ready. Do not require a parent to restate the customer's\n          question or explicitly state the customer's requested conclusion.\n          For example, an explicit statutory maximum or fixed policy limit is\n          response-ready for a question about raising that limit, even if the\n          parent does not separately say \"no\" or describe every possible\n          external arrangement.\n          Preserve each parent's scope and do not infer facts beyond it.\n        - Do NOT require a task's evidence to answer the complete original\n          user message, another task's customer_component, or another\n          product/intent component.\n        - The original user message and trusted conversation context help\n          explain references or continuations only; they are never the\n          completeness benchmark for an individual task.\n        - Return one result for every supplied task_id.\n        - For SUFFICIENT or PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT, return only parent IDs from\n          that exact task that materially support the answer. At least one ID\n          is required for either status.\n        - For PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT or INSUFFICIENT, return an `evidence_delta`\n          list. This is internal evidence-only retrieval context for the intent\n          formulator, not a query, diagnosis, or rewrite instruction. The\n          backend determines terminal clarification after the configured retry limit.\n        - Include an evidence_delta item only when a supplied candidate parent\n          contains a materially task-relevant factual proposition that is NOT\n          already explicit in that task's customer_component. Each item must\n          be exactly {\"parent_id\": \"a supplied candidate parent ID\", \"fact\":\n          \"one concise proposition faithfully supported by that parent\"}.\n        - Never include a restatement, synonym, or reformulation of the\n          customer_component; what candidate parents do not contain; proposed\n          metadata filters, retry stages, angles, query terms, sources,\n          services, or next actions; or any fact outside the supplied parent.\n        - Never use \"search\", \"find\", \"look up\", \"list\", or other imperative\n          wording in evidence_delta facts.\n        - If no new materially relevant candidate fact exists, return an empty\n          evidence_delta list. Do not substitute a generic sentence.\n        - evidence_delta is internal diagnostic context only. It is never\n          approved answer evidence.\n        - Never invent a parent ID, fact, or instruction.\n\n        Output JSON only: {\"task_results\": [{\"task_id\": \"...\", \"status\": \"SUFFICIENT\" | \"PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT\" | \"INSUFFICIENT\", \"approved_parent_ids\": [\"...\"], \"reason\": \"...\", \"evidence_delta\": [{\"parent_id\": \"...\", \"fact\": \"...\"}], \"likely_ambiguous_request\": true|false, \"unresolved_term\": \"\", \"clarification_candidate_replies\": [{\"label\": \"...\", \"message\": \"...\", \"reason\": \"...\"}]}]}\n        "}
